"Procedural Knowledge Improves Agentic LLM Workflows (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.07568v1)\nciteturn1999view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 43; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1996view1\",\"lineno\":255}); Total lines: 1888\nL207@P4: 22 \"file2\": \"files/tools_specification.txt\" } } }\nL208@P4: Figure 1: Top: An in-progress HTN decomposition tree for the Travel Planner problem of §1. White boxes\nL209@P4: indicate abstract tasks, round ovals indicate methods that decompose tasks, and gray boxes indicate\nL210@P4: primitive tasks for the LLM to execute. The dashed box for κ1 indicates it has passed verify. Bottom:\nL211@P4: Notional methods for the same problem.\nL212@P4-5: 5For our domains, we define a function verify : S → {0, 1}, that identifies terminal states (i.e.,\nL213@P5: verify(s) = 1 if s ∈ Sterm and 0 otherwise). The reward function is then:\nL214@P5: r(s,a) = (\nL215@P5: rsuccess if verify(T (s,a)) = 1 (a terminal state)\nL216@P5: rstep otherwise\nL217@P5: where rsuccess = 1 is a positive reward for task completion and rstep is a negative reward for non-terminal\nL218@P5: steps (e.g., rstep = −0.1). Primitive tasks from the HTN are linked to Sterm, which can be verified using\nL219@P5: verify.\nL220@P5: An agent’s behavior is described by a stochastic policy π : S×A → [0, 1], where π(s, a) = P(ai = a | si = s)\nL221@P5: is the probability of selecting a in state s. A solution to M is a policy π that maximizes the expected\nL222@P5: reward (in this case a policy that reaches a terminal state in the fewest steps).\nL223@P5: Initial Analysis. In this work, we investigate the use of an agentic LLM system as the policy π that\nL224@P5: operates within this MDP framework. We show that divide-and-conquer mechanisms such as task\nL225@P5: decomposition can be used to constrain π to work on smaller problems, increasing the expected reward\nL226@P5: obtained by our agentic LLM.\nL227@P5: The general MDP model allows us to hypothesize about why we expect this approach to work. We assume\nL228@P5: that there exists a task-aware assembly function A : S × K → C and let Ci be the context produced from\nL229@P5: state si used as input to the LLM at each iteration.\nL230@P5: Task Success Rate. A task succeeds if a terminal state s ∈ Sterm is reached within H steps. Let\nL231@P5: (a0, . . . , aH−1) be a sequence of actions that arrives at a terminal state from an initial state s0. As\u0002suming deterministic transitions, the probability of taking these actions depends solely on the LLM’s\nL232@P5: parameterization θ, which governs action selection:\nL233@P5: Pθ(a0, a1, . . . , aH−1) =\nL234@P5: H\nL235@P5: Y−1\nL236@P5: i=0\nL237@P5: Pθ(ai|Ci)\nL238@P5: Here, Pθ(ai|Ci) represents the LLM’s conditional probability of selecting action ai given context Ci.\nL239@P5: This factorization arises because each action depends only on the current context (Markov property).\nL240@P5: Task Decomposition. A task decomposition splits a problem into a sequence of smaller subtasks,\nL241@P5: (κ0, . . . , κn−1). Let Pθ(κj ) be the probability of successfully completing subtask j. Decomposition\nL242@P5: improves task success if:\nL243@P5: Pθ(full task) <\nL244@P5: nY−1\nL245@P5: j=0\nL246@P5: Pθ(κj )\nL247@P5: We hypothesize that we can improve the right side of this equation through contextual scaffolding. We\nL248@P5: assume that the LLM’s ability to complete a subtask Pθ(κj ) increases by reducing context complexity.\nL249@P5: When the agent works on a specific subtask κj , the assembly function A(si, κj ) generates a focused\nL250@P5: context C\nL251@P5: ′\nL252@P5: i,j . By constraining the problem space through reducing context complexity, the conditional\nL253@P5: probability of selecting a correct action ai should increase:\nL254@P5: Pθ(ai|C\nL255@P5: ′\nL256@P5: i,j ) > Pθ(ai\nL257@P5: |Ci)\nL258@P5: Because the probability of success for a subtask κj , Pθ(κj ), is the product of the individual Pθ(ai|C\nL259@P5: ′\nL260@P5: i,j ),\nL261@P5: the task success rate Pθ(κj ) will also increase.\nL262@P5: 4 ProcLLM: Embedding Procedural knowledge into an agentic LLM\nL263@P5: ProcLLM implements a planning and acting system (8) in which an agent iterates between HTN planning,\nL264@P5: where it updates the current (sub)task based on progress, and action execution, where it executes\nL265@P5: primitive tasks (i.e., actions) and verifies that they are completed. Relating ProcLLM to the formalism,\nL266@P5: we next describe the components of this system, as shown in Figure 2.\nL267@P5: Environment and states S. For the benchmarks we study in this paper, the environment corresponds\nL268@P5: to a file system with text files and python code files (Figure 2, bottom in yellow). The agent is provided\nL269@P5-6: 6with the following text (.txt) files or python (.py) code, where the first column rwa indicates allowed\nL270@P6: read/write/append actions, respectively.\nL271@P6: r ProblemSpec outlines the domain-specific rules of the problem to solve and format of Answer, which\nL272@P6: is the same for all request instances. (See §C for full details.)\nL273@P6: r Request contains problem instance details to be solved such as the initial configuration of the\nL274@P6: problem and the goal configuration. (See §C for full details.)\nL275@P6: r ToolsSpec is a human readable description of Tools, which are APIs that include source code,\nL276@P6: informational text files, or structured text (e.g., csv, JSON).\nL277@P6: rwa Notes starts empty and will contain the agent’s progress or details about the task or task history.\nL278@P6: rwa Solver is only provided for some problems; the environment executes it, writing to output and error.\nL279@P6: rwa Answer starts empty and will contain the agent’s solution to the overall task.\nL280@P6: The content of the files as well as other details are assembled into context C = A(e1, e2, ..., ek) containing\nL281@P6: one or more elements ei. For ProcLLM this includes the action context Ca = Aa(s,trace, κ)), and the\nL282@P6: verify context Cv = Av(s, trace, κ).\nL283@P6: Algorithm 1 A general procedure for ProcLLM.\nL284@P6: Input: Task Sequence K, Methods M, horizon H\nL285@P6: 1: procedure ProcLLM(K, M, H)\nL286@P6: 2: UpdateTask (K, M)\nL287@P6: 3: for i in 1, ..., H do\nL288@P6: 4: if K == ∅ then break\nL289@P6: 5: κ ← head(K)\nL290@P6: 6: Ca ← Aa(s,trace, κ)\nL291@P6: 7: a(params) ← action-LLM(Ca)\nL292@P6: 8: trace ← ‘’\nL293@P6: 9: if a = verify then\nL294@P6: 10: Cv ← Av(s,κ)\nL295@P6: 11: verified, feedback ← verify-LLM(Cv)\nL296@P6: 12: trace ← feedback\nL297@P6: 13: if verified then\nL298@P6: 14: pop(K) ▷ remove first task κ from K\nL299@P6: 15: UpdateTask (K, M)\nL300@P6: 16: else ▷ apply r/w/a to env’t; (performs s\nL301@P6: ′ ← T (s,a))\nL302@P6: 17: trace ← file ▷ write file to the trace\nL303@P6: 18: if a = write(file, content) then file.clear()\nL304@P6: 19: file ← file + content\nL305@P6: 20: if file = Solver then\nL306@P6: 21: out, err ← Exec(Solver)\nL307@P6: 22: trace ← trace + out + err\nL308@P6: 23: procedure UpdateTask(K, M)\nL309@P6: 24: m ← FindFirstRelevantMethod(head(K),M)\nL310@P6: 25: if m then\nL311@P6: 26: push(m.subtasks, K) ▷ Prepend m.subtasks\nL312@P6: 27: UpdateTask (K, M) ▷ Decompose further\nL313@P6: Actions A and transition T . The agent can take one of four actions: verify, read, write, or append.\nL314@P6: The internal action verify calls a process that checks if the current task has been satisfied, returning a\nL315@P6: boolean verified if the task is completed as well as feedback which is added to the trace. The verify\nL316@P6: step is domain-specific and uses the verify-LLM. (A detailed example of the verify prompt is provided\nL317@P6: in Appendix F.)\nL318@P6: The external actions modify the writeable files, which are subsequently assembled into the context.\nL319@P6: These include:\nL320@P6: read (file): copies file into Notes\nL321@P6: write (file, content): overwrites file with content\nL322@P6: append (file, content): appends content to file\nL323@P6: These external actions must be in a JSON format for the environment to execute (for a detailed example,\nL324@P6-7: 7Figure 2: System overview, labeled with components of the MDP. Blue boxes denote text files, orange\nL325@P7: boxes denote LLMs, red boxes denote python files, and green boxes denote API files (scripts/databas\u0002es/etc.).\nL326@P7-8: 8see Appendix B.2). The environment implements a transition function as a set of scripts that calls\nL327@P8: Exec(Solver) and otherwise modifies files as indicated in the actions.\nL328@P8: Tasks K and Methods M. For ProcLLM, the tasks and methods are domain-dependent. Most domains\nL329@P8: will have the task process user request, which is the top level task that the agent will start with. Figure 1\nL330@P8: shows some simplified methods and their associated sub/tasks; the actual methods and tasks in the\nL331@P8: system’s code differs slightly from this simple form. For the methods in this system, no preconditions\nL332@P8: are specified. In addition to the (head, task, subtask), methods in ProcLLM also include details for the\nL333@P8: verify step.\nL334@P8: As an example, in Figure 1, the most abstract task process user request decomposes into four subtasks\nL335@P8: (Lines 3-6) and the verify context includes additional details (Lines 7-9). The second subtask choose\nL336@P8: departing flight is further decomposed by m-choose-departing-flight (Lines 10-17) where one of the\nL337@P8: subtasks is to understand the flight tool, as described in m-understand-flight-tool (Lines 18-22).\nL338@P8: Workflow of ProcLLM. Figure 2 shows an overview of the system with the key components of the\nL339@P8: system. Along the top of the diagram, moving left to right, are the agentic components that produce an\nL340@P8: action. The bottom of the diagram shows the components that make up the environment.\nL341@P8: Algorithm 1 provides pseudocode for ProcLLM. If methods M are provided, Line 2 calls UpdateTask\nL342@P8: to decompose K. UpdateTask (Lines 23-27) decomposes head(K) if there are relevant methods. If\nL343@P8: verified is True, UpdateTask will remove head(K) and may further decompose K, where the leaves are\nL344@P8: primitive tasks that describe what action-LLM should do. K remains unchanged if verified is False, M\nL345@P8: is empty, or there are no relevant methods for head(K).\nL346@P8: After calling UpdateTask, ProcLLM then processes a sequence of tasks K up to the horizon H (Line 3)\nL347@P8: or until K is empty (Line 4). Line 6 assembles an action context Ca from the current environment\nL348@P8: state s, the execution trace of the last system execution, and the current task κ. Ca is processed by\nL349@P8: action-LLM (Line 7) which produces a parameterized action a, which is either an internal verify action\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSCALAR: Benchmarking SAE Interaction Sparsity in Toy LLMs (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07572v1)\nciteturn1999view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1990view2\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 662\nL81: We sort connections between a pair of SAEs by their importance (measured using integrated gradient attributions cite58†Wang et al. [2024] ) and progressively remove them, measuring the KL divergence between the original model output and the ablated output. We then compute the area under the degradation curve, which quantifies how compact the interaction graph can be while preserving downstream performance.\nL82: To enable fair comparisons across different SAE configurations, we report both absolute and relative interaction sparsity. Absolute sparsity measures the raw area, while relative sparsity normalises area by the total number of connections between SAE pairs. Each metric on its own can be misleading: relative sparsity can be inflated by adding inactive features, and absolute sparsity can favor small but densely connected dictionaries.\nL83: ##### Contributions\nL84: \nL85: Our main contributions in this work are as follows:\nL86: \nL87:   * •\nL88: Staircase SAEs: We introduce Staircase SAEs, which use an architectural modification to improve the representation of pass-through circuits. Staircase SAEs simplify pass-through connections by explicitly sharing upstream features across all downstream SAEs. We find that, at any given layer, the active features consist of around 25 - 45% new features and 55 - 75% features from previous layers. This is compatible with the results of cite59†Balcells et al.\nL89: [2024] which studied pass-through circuits in standard SAEs.\nL90:   * •\nL91: \nL92: SCALAR Benchmark: We introduce SCALAR (Sparse Connectivity Assessment of Latent Activation Relationships), a benchmark for quantifying interaction sparsity between pairs of SAEs. SCALAR measures how model performance degrades as inter-layer connections are progressively ablated in order of importance, offering a principled measure of circuit simplicity.\nL93: \nL94:   * •\nL95: Empirical Validation: We use SCALAR to evaluate multiple SAE designs and find a tradeoff between standard SAEs, which may perform well initially but degrade under ablation, and Jacobian or Staircase SAEs, which retain performance even under pruning.\nL96: ## 2 Related Work\nL97: ##### SAE Architectures and Cross-Layer Interactions\nL98: Our work builds on research using SAEs to interpret neural networks [cite50†Cunningham et al., 2023 , cite49†Bricken et al., 2023 , cite60†Gao et al., 2024 ]. Current SAE methods face issues like feature splitting, absorption, and incompleteness that vary across layers, leading to inconsistencies in circuit interpretation. To address cross-layer interactions, cite55†Farnik et al. [2025] introduced Jacobian SAEs (JSAEs) that minimize interaction sparsity via L1 Jacobian penalties, though limited to feedforward layers.\nL99: cite61†Yun et al. [2023] and cite52†Lindsey et al. [2024] proposed shared SAEs and crosscoders, while cite53†Dunefsky et al. [2024] introduced transcoders for cross-layer reconstruction.\nL100: ##### Focus on Single-Layer Reconstruction\nL101: \nL102: Although shared architectures and crosscoders yield sparse feature dictionaries across layers, they obscure individual layer representations. Since our goal is understanding computation flow within networks, we focus on approaches that reconstruct activations at single layers while improving cross-layer sparsity through architectural innovations rather than abandoning per-layer interpretability.\nL103: ## 3 Methodology\nL104: We use a toy language model to benchmark the interaction sparsity of different SAE architectures: A 216K-parameter transformer [cite62†Vaswani et al., 2023 ] following the GPT-2 architecture [cite63†Radford et al., 2019 ]. The model has 4 layers, a residual stream width of 64, and uses an ASCII tokenizer (vocabulary size of 128) similar to cite64†Lai and Heimersheim [2025] .\nL105: We use a DynamicTanh layer [cite65†Zhu et al., 2025 ] instead of LayerNorm to make computing the Jacobian for JSAEs tractable (see cite32†Section D.1 ). We train the model on the tiny-Shakespeare dataset [cite66†Karpathy, 2015b , cite67†Karpathy, 2015a ] to perform standard next-token prediction.\nL106: SAEs are trained to reconstruct model activations that are mapped to and from a sparse high dimensional space [cite50†Cunningham et al., 2023 , cite49†Bricken et al., 2023 ]. We train SAEs on activations between transformer blocks, and on the input/output of each feedforward layer. In all cases, the activations are vectors of the same size as the residual stream. We train three types of SAEs: Conventional TopK SAEs cite60†Gao et al.\nL107: [2024] , Jacobian SAEs [cite55†Farnik et al., 2025 , JSAEs], and our new Staircase SAEs.\nL108: ### 3.1 TopK SAEs\nL109: \nL110: TopK SAEs use a TopK activation function which selects the $K$ largest magnitude entries in the latent vector and sets all other entries to zero.\nL111: \nL112:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}=\\text{TopK}(\\text{ReLU}(\\mathbf{W}_{enc}(\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{b}_{dec})+\\mathbf{b}_{enc})),\\quad\\mathbf{z^{\\prime}}=\\mathbf{W}_{dec}\\mathbf{z}+\\mathbf{b}_{dec}$  |  | (1)\nL113: Sparsity is enforced directly by the TopK operation, ensuring by definition^{1}^{1} 1 It may be less than $K$, due the ReLU. that $||\\mathbf{z}||_{0}\\leq K$. The SAEs are trained on the reconstruction loss\nL114: \nL115:  | $\\displaystyle L_{\\rm TopK\\ SAE}=||\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{h}^{\\prime}||_{2}^{2}.$  |  | (2)\nL116: \nL117: We train TopK SAEs independently for each position in the model.\nL118: ### 3.2 Jacobian SAEs\nL119: \nL120: JSAEs [cite55†Farnik et al., 2025 ] sparsify the interactions between latents, in addition to the latents themselves. This is done by imposing an L1 penalty on the Jacobian of downstream latents with respect to upstream latents.\nL121: \nL122:  | $$\\displaystyle L_{\\rm Jac}=\\|J\\|_{1},\\quad J_{ij}=\\frac{\\partial z^{\\rm downstream}_{i}(\\mathbf{z^{\\rm upstream}})}{\\partial z^{\\rm upstream}_{j}}.$$  |  | (3)\nL123: Due to the sparsity of the latents, the Jacobian itself is sparse, and for a pair of TopK SAEs trained across a feedforward layer, there are only at most $K^{2}$ non-zero entries. These entries can be computed cheaply in closed form, see cite32†Section D.1 . JSAEs are trained with a mixture of both losses:\nL124: \nL125:  | $\\displaystyle L_{\\rm JSAE}=L_{\\rm TopK\\ SAE}+\\lambda L_{\\rm Jac}$  |  | (4)\nL126: Applying JSAEs to standard Transformer feedforward blocks that use LayerNorm (LN) [cite68†Ba et al., 2016 ] presents a challenge due to LN’s inherent dependencies between elements of the input vector.\nL127: \nL128:  | $\\displaystyle{\\rm FFBlock}(x)=x+{\\rm FF}({\\rm LN}(x))$  |  | (5)\nL129: These dependencies complicate the modification of the efficient closed-form of $J$ for feedforward blocks, resulting in a large intermediate term when computing the Jacobian (cite31†Appendix D ). In contrast, DynamicTanh (DyT) [cite65†Zhu et al., 2025 ] is an element-wise operation, that can be used as a drop-in replacement for LN. We fine-tune our model to substitute LN layers with DyT (cite30†Appendix C ), modify the derivation of $J$ in cite55†Farnik et al.\nL130: [2025] to incorporate both DyT and the skip connection, and utilize this Jacobian to train JSAEs over MLP blocks.\nL131: ### 3.3 Staircase SAEs\nL132: \nL133: We introduce the Staircase SAE, an architecture aimed at promoting feature reuse from earlier layers to aid in activation reconstruction. This approach seeks to facilitate circuit sparsity through structural design rather than encouraging it via an explicit training objective (as for JSAEs).\nL134: Staircase SAEs form a collection of related SAEs that use shared weights. All SAEs use the same encoder and decoder weight matrices, $\\mathbf{W}_{enc}$ and $\\mathbf{W}_{dec}$. Each SAE layer $i$ (for $1\\leq i\\leq L+1$) accesses a progressively larger slice of these shared weights. Assuming a base feature chunk size of $n$, the feature dimension for an SAE on layer $i$ is $n\\times i$. The encoder and decoder weights for layer $i$ are:\nL135:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{W}^{i}_{enc}:=\\mathbf{W}_{enc}[:\\;ni,:]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\text{model}}\\times ni},\\quad\\mathbf{W}^{i}_{dec}:=\\mathbf{W}_{dec}[:,:ni]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{ni\\times d_{\\text{model}}}$  |  | (6)\nL136: where $\\mathbf{W}_{enc}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\text{model}}\\times N}$ and $\\mathbf{W}_{dec}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times d_{\\text{model}}}$ are the full shared matrices (see cite56†Figure 2 ). The notation $\\mathbf{M}[:k,:]$ selects the first $k$ rows of $\\mathbf{M}$; $\\mathbf{M}[:,:k]$ selects the first $k$ columns. Each successive layer thus gains access to all earlier features, plus an additional chunk of $n$ new features.\nL137: cite69†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: The staircase SAE architecture for a transformer with $L=3$ layers. Each layer $i$ uses a slice of the shared encoder $\\mathbf{W}_{enc}$ and decoder $\\mathbf{W}_{dec}$ weights. SAE chunks of identical colour indicate weights shared within the slices $\\mathbf{W}^{i}_{enc}$ and $\\mathbf{W}^{i}_{dec}$.\nL138: cite70†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: By measuring the number of active latents per chunk, we can see feature reuse from previous layers, as each SAEs allocates some “sparsity budget” to features from previous layers.\nL139: Crucially, the biases $\\mathbf{b}_{enc}^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{ni}$ and $\\mathbf{b}_{dec}^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\text{model}}}$ are independent for each layer $i$. Independent decoder biases $\\mathbf{b}_{dec}^{i}$ allow each SAE to center activations appropriately for its layer.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLM Output Drift: Cross-Provider Validation & Mitigation for Financial Workflows (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07585v1)\nciteturn1999view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view0\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 660\nL84: These incidents align with our economic analysis of verification overhead, as deployments like Chronograph’s Claude integration for private equity portfolio analysis (cite55†Digital Wealth News, 2025 ) demand deterministic controls to prevent drift in high-stakes financial decisions.\nL85: The economic implications are substantial. Morgan Stanley estimates $920 billion in potential savings from AI automation of financial knowledge work (cite56†Morgan Stanley Research, 2025 ), yet as industry observer Gene Marks notes: “Whatever time people are spending using AI is offset by them actually verifying what AI did because no one yet trusts AI” (cite57†Marks, 2025 ). This verification overhead negates automation benefits unless deterministic outputs can be guaranteed.\nL86: Our key empirical finding: Determinism is not universal across model architectures. Evaluating five models across 480 runs (n=16 per condition), we demonstrate that well-engineered smaller models (7-8B parameters) achieve perfect output consistency, while 120B parameter models fail at 12.5% consistency even with identical configuration ($T{=}0.0$, greedy decoding, fixed seeds).\nL87: This inverse correlation between model scale and determinism fundamentally alters deployment strategies for regulated applications, where audit requirements mandate reproducibility over raw capability. Table cite58†1 summarizes our model tier classification for financial AI deployment based on empirical consistency measurements.\nL88: Table 1. Model Tiers for Financial Compliance: Deployment Decision Matrix\nL89: \nL90: Tier  | Models  | Consistency  | Compliance  | Use Cases\nL91: Tier 1  | 7-8B  | 100%  | Full  | All regulated tasks\nL92: Tier 2  | 40-70B  | 56-100%  | Limited  | Structured only\nL93: Tier 3  | 120B  | 12.5%  | Requires validation  | Non-compliant\nL94: \nL95: Note: n=480 total runs (16 runs per condition across 5 models × 3 tasks × 2 temperatures); Tier 1 models (Granite-3-8B, Qwen2.5:7B) achieve audit-ready determinism at $T{=}0.0$\nL96: Why Financial Services Require Different AI Standards: Financial AI systems operate under unique constraints that distinguish them from general-purpose applications. First, regulatory frameworks (Basel III, Dodd-Frank, MiFID II)(cite59†for International Settlements, 2025 ; cite60†Congress, 2010 ; cite61†Union, 2014 ) mandate explainable and consistent decision-making for credit, trading, and risk management.\nL97: Second, financial institutions face strict audit requirements where AI-driven decisions must be reproducible months or years later for regulatory examination. Third, the high-stakes nature of financial decisions-affecting customer creditworthiness, investment recommendations, and market stability-demands reliability levels far exceeding consumer applications.\nL98: Finally, cross-border operations require AI systems to demonstrate consistent behavior across different regulatory jurisdictions and infrastructure deployments.\nL99: Example: reconciliation analysts re-verify AI-produced break explanations, turning drift into direct rework hours.\nL100: \nL101: Our experimental investigation addresses this gap with four key contributions:\nL102: \nL103:   * •\nL104: \nL105: Empirical proof of achievable determinism: We demonstrate 100% output consistency at $T{=}0.0$ across varying concurrency levels, establishing feasibility for production financial systems\nL106: \nL107:   * •\nL108: Quantified drift patterns: We measure task-specific sensitivity to randomness, revealing that SQL generation maintains determinism even at $T{=}0.2$ while RAG tasks show substantial drift (25-75% consistency at $T{=}0.2$)\nL109: \nL110:   * •\nL111: \nL112: Practical mitigation framework: We provide a tiered control system with measured effectiveness, enabling risk-appropriate deployment strategies\nL113: \nL114:   * •\nL115: Finance-calibrated validation protocol: We introduce domain-specific adaptations including SEC 10-K structure-aware retrieval ordering, finance-calibrated tolerance thresholds (±5%) as acceptance gates, and bi-temporal audit trails mapped to FSB/CFTC requirements—innovations requiring financial regulatory expertise beyond general ML reproducibility techniques\nL116: We quantify how concurrency and sampler settings affect output drift in tasks common to financial workflows (RAG, summarization, SQL). We also show how application-level controls—seeded decoding, retrieval-order normalization, and schema-constrained outputs—reduce drift without relying solely on infrastructure fixes. Finally, we connect these controls to operational and regulatory guidance for financial institutions.\nL117: Recent incident reports documenting intermittent answer variance at production scale and research on batch-invariant kernels address infra-level nondeterminism. Our results complement these by isolating and mitigating application-layer contributors to drift in finance workflows, offering controls that are deployable today.\nL118: ## 2. Related Work\nL119: ### 2.1. Technical Foundations of Nondeterminism\nL120: The breakthrough understanding of LLM nondeterminism comes from Thinking Machines Lab’s September 2025 work (cite50†Thinking Machines Lab, 2025 ), which identified batch-size variation-not floating-point arithmetic-as the primary cause. Their batch-invariant kernels achieve exact reproducibility by ensuring operations yield identical results regardless of batch composition.\nL121: Recent work on SLM agents (cite62†Belcak and others, 2025 ) complements batch-invariant kernels by advocating smaller models for efficient, deterministic agentic inference. Complementary research on deterministic decoding methods (cite63†Shi et al., 2024 ) and hardware-level approaches including HPC reproducibility studies (cite64†Antunes and Hill, 2021 ) provides additional foundations. This finding shows that determinism is achievable through engineering rather than fundamentally impossible.\nL122: Baldwin et al. (cite65†Baldwin and others, 2024 ) quantified the problem’s severity, demonstrating accuracy variations up to 15% across runs even at zero temperature, with performance gaps reaching 70% between best and worst outcomes. They introduced the Total Agreement Rate (TAR) metric, providing the quantitative framework we extend in our financial context evaluation.\nL123: Recent research identifies additional practical nondeterminism sources at $T{=}0.0$, including non-batch-invariant matrix multiplication and attention kernels causing numeric divergence under varying loads. Tokenizer drift—where text normalization changes inflate token counts by up to 112% and enable command injection vulnerabilities—further exacerbates output variability and operational costs (cite66†Trend Micro Research, 2025 ).\nL124: Analysis of model drift in crisis response contexts (cite67†The New Humanitarian, 2025 ) shows subtle output shifts can alter narratives, paralleling financial compliance requirements for stable, auditable responses. State-of-the-art LLM observability tools (cite68†Braintrust, 2025 ) emphasize real-time monitoring of prompt-response pairs to detect such issues.\nL125: The vLLM serving infrastructure (cite69†Kwon and others, 2023 ) with PagedAttention provides efficient batching that can be extended with deterministic kernels. Production deployments of models like Qwen with vLLM (cite70†Red Hat, 2025 ) demonstrate the framework’s maturity, though as we demonstrate, efficient serving alone doesn’t guarantee consistency-explicit determinism controls are required.\nL126: ### 2.2. Financial AI Requirements and Benchmarks\nL127: Recent financial AI benchmarks-FinBen (cite71†Xie and others, 2024 ), SEC-QA (cite72†Lai et al., 2024 ), and DocFinQA (cite73†Reddy and others, 2024 )-focus on accuracy metrics while overlooking reproducibility. While FinBen overlooks reproducibility, SLM agent frameworks (cite62†Belcak and others, 2025 ) suggest fine-tuned small models could enhance consistency in financial QA. SEC-QA’s 1% margin tolerance for numerical answers and DocFinQA’s 123,000-word contexts test reasoning capability but not output stability.\nL128: Existing financial AI benchmarks evaluate accuracy but not reproducibility—a fundamental gap for regulated deployments. A model achieving 95% accuracy on SEC-QA but exhibiting 25% output variance across runs fails regulatory requirements despite strong performance metrics. Our work addresses this overlooked dimension, demonstrating that model selection for compliance requires determinism evaluation alongside traditional accuracy benchmarks.\nL129: The regulatory landscape demands more.\nL130: The Financial Stability Board (cite74†Financial Stability Board, 2024 ; cite75†Financial Stability Board, 2017 ) requires “consistent and traceable decision-making,” the Bank for International Settlements (cite76†Bank for International Settlements, 2024 ; cite77†Prenio and Yong, 2021 ) mandates “clear accountability across the AI lifecycle,” and CFTC guidance (cite78†CFTC, 2024 ; cite79†Commodity Futures Trading Commission, Technology Advisory Committee, 2024 ) demands “proper documentation of AI system outcomes.” Additional regulatory oversight comes from the Federal Reserve (cite80†Federal Reserve Board, 2024 ) and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (cite81†Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, 2025 ).\nL131: Our work bridges the gap between these requirements and technical capabilities, incorporating reproducibility guidance from PyTorch and vLLM (cite82†PyTorch Team, 2025 ; cite83†vLLM Team, 2025 ).\n"
